From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Subject: [PATCH] build on forward-ported pre-4.0 kernels

Upstream gates this module off below Linux 4.0 with a hard #error, because it
uses the idr allocator, timespec64/ktime_get_ts64, list_first_entry_or_null,
the modern colorspace/buffer-timestamp/event-subscription enumerators, struct
v4l2_subscribed_event_ops and the video_device vfl_dir/dev_debug fields - none
of which the stock pre-4.0 V4L2 core has.

On LuneOS the old Halium device kernels that need a /dev/video bridge
(droidcam2v4l2) have those symbols backported additively into their V4L2 core
(the per-device shr-distribution/linux branches). With that in place the only
thing the kernel cannot provide is mapping the modern four-argument
v4l2_event_subscribe() onto the arity its core actually keeps, because the
signature changed across the 2.6.29 -> 4.0 range these kernels span. Relax the
version gate and, below 4.0, map the call: a forward-ported kernel defines
V4L2L_SUBSCRIBE_TAKES_ELEMS (in <media/v4l2-event.h>) when its core function is
the three-argument (fh, sub, elems) form, otherwise the oldest two-argument form
is used. The per-subscription element count and the (here unused) event ops are
handled kernel-side.

The change is confined to LINUX_VERSION_CODE < 4.0.0, so builds on 4.0+ kernels
(every other Halium device using this recipe) are byte-for-byte unaffected.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [upstream intentionally requires kernel >= 4.0]
---
 v4l2loopback.c | 25 +++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/v4l2loopback.c
+++ b/v4l2loopback.c
@@ -39,7 +39,26 @@
 #define V4L2LOOPBACK_CTL_QUERY_legacy 0x4C82
 
 #if LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(4, 0, 0)
-#error This module is not supported on kernels before 4.0.0.
+/* LuneOS: build on the forward-ported pre-4.0 Halium device kernels, whose
+ * V4L2 core has been extended additively with the idr allocator, timespec64 /
+ * ktime_get_ts64, list_first_entry_or_null, the colorspace / buffer-timestamp /
+ * event-subscription enumerators, device_caps, struct v4l2_subscribed_event_ops
+ * and the video_device vfl_dir / dev_debug fields this module uses (see the
+ * per-device shr-distribution/linux branches). The one thing that cannot live
+ * in the kernel is matching the modern four-argument v4l2_event_subscribe() to
+ * the arity that tree's core actually keeps, which varies across the
+ * 2.6.29 -> 4.0 span these kernels cover. A forward-ported kernel advertises it
+ * through <media/v4l2-event.h>: V4L2L_SUBSCRIBE_TAKES_OPS when the core already
+ * has the four-argument form (call it directly), V4L2L_SUBSCRIBE_TAKES_ELEMS
+ * for the three-argument (fh, sub, elems) form, else the oldest two-argument
+ * form. The per-subscription ops are handled kernel-side and dropped here. */
+#ifdef V4L2L_SUBSCRIBE_TAKES_OPS
+/* core already takes (fh, sub, elems, ops); no remapping needed */
+#elif defined(V4L2L_SUBSCRIBE_TAKES_ELEMS)
+#define v4l2_event_subscribe(fh, sub, elems, ops) (v4l2_event_subscribe)(fh, sub, elems)
+#else
+#define v4l2_event_subscribe(fh, sub, elems, ops) (v4l2_event_subscribe)(fh, sub)
+#endif
 #endif
 
 #if LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(4, 3, 0)
